Message-ID: <qwIycHYRka4.w2Zw_474UlwJ@statforling-with-r.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <qwIycHYRka4.JtbCWVYAQJcJ@statforling-with-r.groups.google.com>
References: <qwIycHYRka4.JtbCWVYAQJcJ@statforling-with-r.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/statforling-with-r/c/qwIycHYRka4/m/w2Zw_474UlwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This can best be answered with code:

# set up
rm(list=ls(all=TRUE))
library(Design)
logit<-function(x) { log(x/(1-x)) }
ilogit<-function(x) { 1/(1+exp(-x)) }

# generate random data
set.seed(3)
x1 <- factor(sample(letters[1:3], 500, replace=TRUE))
x2 <- factor(sample(letters[4:6], 500, replace=TRUE))
x3 <- factor(sample(letters[8:10], 500, replace=TRUE))
y <-factor(sample(letters[25:26], 500, replace=TRUE))

# fit a model
m1 <- lrm(y ~ x1 + x2 + x3)

# inspect predictions
ilogit(coef(m1)) # inverse logit of the intercept =
tapply(predict(m1, type="fitted.ind"), list(x1, x2, x3), mean)[1,1,1]
# the predicted probability when all independent vars are at their
first level

Cheers,
